ROOT=Path.cwd();ART=ROOT/"articles";BACK=ROOT/"backup-bloco-03-artigos";BACK.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
DATA=[{'filename': 'como-aprovechar-el-alfeizar-de-las-ventanas.html', 'title': 'How to Make the Most of Your Windowsill and Turn It Into Useful Space', 'desc': 'Practical ideas for using a windowsill for decoration, suitable plants, light storage, and everyday function while preserving natural light and access.', 'intro': 'A windowsill can become a useful part of a room when its dimensions, natural light, window operation, and maintenance needs are considered carefully.', 'category': 'HOME', 'body': "<h2>In This Article</h2><p>A windowsill is often treated as leftover space, yet with careful planning it can become a decorative surface, a place for suitable plants, or a small functional feature without reducing light, ventilation, or window access.</p><h2>Introduction</h2><p>A windowsill is often treated as leftover space, yet with careful planning it can become a decorative surface, a place for suitable plants, or a small functional feature without reducing light, ventilation, or window access. Careful measurement, clear priorities, and simple maintenance are the foundation of a result that remains useful over time.</p>\n<h2>Check the Measurements</h2><p>Measure the width, depth, and usable height of the windowsill. Check handles, inward-opening panels, curtains, blinds, and screens. The arrangement must never prevent normal operation of the window. Also consider the strength of the surface and keep heavy objects away when its load capacity is uncertain.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Use It as a Decorative Zone</h2><p>A restrained arrangement of a vase, frame, books, or another decorative object can add character without consuming floor space. Vary heights slightly, repeat materials or colors already present in the room, and leave visible gaps so the composition feels intentional rather than crowded.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Consider Suitable Plants</h2><p>Plants can work well when the window provides the light their species needs. Observe direct and indirect sunlight before choosing them. Use stable pots proportional to the ledge, protect the surface from moisture, and never allow watering to damage wood, paint, stone, or nearby finishes.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Preserve Natural Light</h2><p>The main purpose of a window includes bringing daylight into the room. Avoid blocking a small window with tall or dense objects. Lower arrangements and visually light pieces can add interest while allowing daylight to remain useful.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Keep Ventilation Easy</h2><p>Frequently opened windows need clear access to handles and moving panels. Objects should be easy to remove and stable enough not to fall when air enters. A simple display is usually better than an elaborate one when the window is used every day.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Think About Heat and Temperature</h2><p>Direct sun, cold glass, drafts, and seasonal temperature changes can affect plants and decorative objects. Observe the ledge at different times of day before placing sensitive items there permanently.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Use Light Storage Carefully</h2><p>A deep, structurally suitable sill may hold a small tray for lightweight items, but it should not become overflow storage. Limit the contents to objects that benefit from being nearby and keep the window easy to clean.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Adapt the Idea to the Room</h2><p>A kitchen window may suit a few appropriate herbs, while a bedroom may benefit from a calmer display. Bathrooms require attention to humidity and privacy, and children's rooms require extra care with heavy, breakable, sharp, or easily pulled objects.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Use the View as Part of the Design</h2><p>Consider what you see beyond the window. A low arrangement can frame an attractive view, while selected plants can soften a less attractive outlook without eliminating daylight. Also consider how the display appears from outside.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Make Cleaning Simple</h2><p>Windowsills collect dust, condensation, pollen, and outdoor particles. Choose objects that can be removed quickly. Inspect beneath pots and trays for moisture and clean the surface regularly.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Rotate Instead of Accumulating</h2><p>Store some decorative pieces and rotate them occasionally rather than displaying everything at once. This keeps the area fresh without creating clutter or requiring constant new purchases.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Avoid Common Mistakes</h2><p>Do not block window mechanisms, use oversized pots, leave standing water beneath plants, place heat-sensitive objects in intense sun, or cover the entire ledge with unrelated pieces. Function comes before decoration.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Create Visual Balance</h2><p>Begin with one main object and one or two supporting pieces. Step back and evaluate the result from across the room. Balance can come from shared colors, materials, or shapes and does not require perfect symmetry.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Plan Before Shopping</h2><p>Test objects you already own before buying new accessories. Once you know which proportions and colors work, purchase only what is genuinely missing and fits the measurements.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Review the Arrangement Seasonally</h2><p>Sunlight and temperature change during the year. Plants or delicate objects that work well in one season may need to move in another. A flexible arrangement is easier to maintain than a permanent display.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>Should every available area be used?</h3><p>No. Empty space has practical value because it improves access, cleaning, light, and visual order. Use an area only when the new function genuinely improves daily life.</p>\n<h3>Do I need expensive custom solutions?</h3><p>Not necessarily. Accurate measurements and well-chosen standard products can solve many problems. Custom work is most useful when unusual dimensions create a genuine limitation.</p>\n<h3>How can I avoid clutter?</h3><p>Give every item a purpose and a defined place. Keep frequently used objects accessible, store occasional items appropriately, and periodically remove things that are no longer useful.</p>\n<h3>What should I do before buying anything?</h3><p>Measure the available width, depth, height, openings, and circulation space. Then decide what function is missing. Shopping after this analysis reduces unsuitable purchases.</p>\n<h3>How do I keep the result easy to maintain?</h3><p>Choose durable and accessible solutions, avoid overcrowding, and keep a simple cleaning and organization routine.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2><p>A well-planned home does not depend on filling every surface. The best improvements respond to real dimensions, daily habits, and maintenance needs. Measure carefully, protect circulation and light, choose useful elements, and leave enough open space for the room to remain comfortable.</p><h2>Plan Changes in Stages</h2><p>Test the layout with what you already own before spending money. Move a piece of furniture, remove an unnecessary object, or mark proposed dimensions on the floor. Living with a temporary arrangement for several days can reveal practical issues that are difficult to notice in a photograph.</p><p>Make functional changes first and decorate afterward. When access, storage, light, and circulation work well, decorative decisions become easier because you know which surfaces can remain free and which areas can support additional elements.</p><h2>Plan Changes in Stages</h2><p>Test the layout with what you already own before spending money. Move a piece of furniture, remove an unnecessary object, or mark proposed dimensions on the floor. Living with a temporary arrangement for several days can reveal practical issues that are difficult to notice in a photograph.</p><p>Make functional changes first and decorate afterward. When access, storage, light, and circulation work well, decorative decisions become easier because you know which surfaces can remain free and which areas can support additional elements.</p><h2>Plan Changes in Stages</h2><p>Test the layout with what you already own before spending money. Move a piece of furniture, remove an unnecessary object, or mark proposed dimensions on the floor. Living with a temporary arrangement for several days can reveal practical issues that are difficult to notice in a photograph.</p><p>Make functional changes first and decorate afterward. When access, storage, light, and circulation work well, decorative decisions become easier because you know which surfaces can remain free and which areas can support additional elements.</p><h2>Plan Changes in Stages</h2><p>Test the layout with what you already own before spending money. Move a piece of furniture, remove an unnecessary object, or mark proposed dimensions on the floor. Living with a temporary arrangement for several days can reveal practical issues that are difficult to notice in a photograph.</p><p>Make functional changes first and decorate afterward. When access, storage, light, and circulation work well, decorative decisions become easier because you know which surfaces can remain free and which areas can support additional elements.</p><h2>Plan Changes in Stages</h2><p>Test the layout with what you already own before spending money. Move a piece of furniture, remove an unnecessary object, or mark proposed dimensions on the floor. Living with a temporary arrangement for several days can reveal practical issues that are difficult to notice in a photograph.</p><p>Make functional changes first and decorate afterward. When access, storage, light, and circulation work well, decorative decisions become easier because you know which surfaces can remain free and which areas can support additional elements.</p>"}, {'filename': 'como-aprovechar-espacios-pequenos-en-casa.html', 'title': 'How to Make the Most of Small Spaces at Home', 'desc': 'Learn how to make small spaces more functional through better layouts, practical furniture, vertical storage, clear circulation, organized zones, and thoughtful lighting.', 'intro': 'Making a small home work well is about using every area intelligently while preserving comfort, circulation, light, and visual order.', 'category': 'HOME', 'body': '<h2>In This Article</h2><p>Making a small home work well does not mean filling it with furniture or sacrificing comfort. The key is understanding how each area is used, preserving circulation, and choosing solutions that provide useful functions without creating clutter.</p><h2>Introduction</h2><p>Making a small home work well does not mean filling it with furniture or sacrificing comfort. The key is understanding how each area is used, preserving circulation, and choosing solutions that provide useful functions without creating clutter. Careful measurement, clear priorities, and simple maintenance are the foundation of a result that remains useful over time.</p>\n<h2>Analyze the Available Space</h2><p>Observe how every room is actually used. Mark doors, windows, fixed installations, electrical points, and natural walking routes. A simple measured sketch can reveal why a room feels crowded and which functions deserve priority.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Choose Functional Furniture</h2><p>Furniture should justify the floor area it occupies. Consider pieces with useful secondary functions such as storage benches, extendable tables, or appropriate under-bed storage. Scale matters as much as function; oversized pieces can make circulation difficult.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Use Vertical Space</h2><p>Shelves, tall cabinets, hooks, and wall-mounted solutions can add storage without consuming as much floor area. Keep frequently used items within comfortable reach and secure wall-mounted furniture appropriately for the wall type.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Protect Circulation</h2><p>Maintain clear routes between doors, beds, seating, work areas, and storage. Remember to test drawers and cabinet doors when open. If people must constantly turn sideways or move furniture to pass, adjust the layout.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Create Zones Without Walls</h2><p>Rugs, lighting, furniture placement, and compact work surfaces can define activities without solid partitions. Keep colors and materials connected so multiple functions still feel like one coherent home.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Use Natural and Artificial Light</h2><p>Avoid unnecessarily blocking windows with tall furniture. Combine general lighting with task lighting where activities such as reading, cooking, or working require it. Mirrors can reflect light, but position them so they do not simply duplicate visual clutter.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Keep Materials Cohesive</h2><p>A limited family of colors and finishes can make connected areas feel calmer. Small rooms do not have to be white; the important point is visual continuity. Repeat selected materials and add texture without introducing too many competing elements.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Organize by Frequency of Use</h2><p>Keep everyday objects in the easiest storage locations and move seasonal or rarely used items higher or farther away. Leave some spare capacity so storage remains easy to use rather than completely packed.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Use Corners Intentionally</h2><p>A corner may support a compact desk, reading chair, plant, or narrow storage unit when dimensions allow. Give it one clear purpose and make sure the new piece does not interfere with doors or common walking routes.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Improve the Bedroom Layout</h2><p>Start with the bed because it usually occupies the most space. Preserve necessary access and make sure wardrobes and drawers open comfortably. Compact bedside solutions can provide essentials without oversized furniture.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Improve a Small Living Room</h2><p>Choose seating for regular household use rather than rare maximum occupancy. Flexible extra seating and smaller movable tables can preserve circulation. Organize media, books, and accessories into coherent zones instead of several bulky pieces.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Make a Small Kitchen Work Better</h2><p>Protect clear work surfaces and keep frequently used tools near their activity zones. Store occasional appliances when practical. Organize cabinet interiors for visibility and access rather than simply packing in the maximum number of objects.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Create a Useful Entryway</h2><p>Give keys, bags, shoes, and outerwear defined places. In a narrow entrance, wall-mounted hooks or a slim shelf may be more practical than a large console table that reduces circulation.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Reduce Visual Clutter</h2><p>Group similar items, use closed storage for visually irregular necessities, and leave some surfaces intentionally clear. Fewer meaningful decorative pieces often create more personality than many small unrelated accessories.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Review the Home Regularly</h2><p>Needs change over time. Periodically reconsider furniture and storage that no longer serve daily life. Before buying something new, decide where it will live and what function it adds or replaces.</p>\n<p>Before making changes in this area, observe how it affects everyday use, cleaning, access, light, and circulation. Measure first and avoid adding objects simply because there appears to be unused space. A solution is successful when it makes the home easier to live in and can be maintained without unnecessary effort.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>Should every available area be used?</h3><p>No. Empty space has practical value because it improves access, cleaning, light, and visual order. Use an area only when the new function genuinely improves daily life.</p>\n<h3>Do I need expensive custom solutions?</h3><p>Not necessarily. Accurate measurements and well-chosen standard products can solve many problems. Custom work is most useful when unusual dimensions create a genuine limitation.</p>\n<h3>How can I avoid clutter?</h3><p>Give every item a purpose and a defined place. Keep frequently used objects accessible, store occasional items appropriately, and periodically remove things that are no longer useful.</p>\n<h3>What should I do before buying anything?</h3><p>Measure the available width, depth, height, openings, and circulation space. Then decide what function is missing. Shopping after this analysis reduces unsuitable purchases.</p>\n<h3>How do I keep the result easy to maintain?</h3><p>Choose durable and accessible solutions, avoid overcrowding, and keep a simple cleaning and organization routine.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2><p>A well-planned home does not depend on filling every surface. The best improvements respond to real dimensions, daily habits, and maintenance needs. Measure carefully, protect circulation and light, choose useful elements, and leave enough open space for the room to remain comfortable.</p><h2>Plan Changes in Stages</h2><p>Test the layout with what you already own before spending money. Move a piece of furniture, remove an unnecessary object, or mark proposed dimensions on the floor. Living with a temporary arrangement for several days can reveal practical issues that are difficult to notice in a photograph.</p><p>Make functional changes first and decorate afterward. When access, storage, light, and circulation work well, decorative decisions become easier because you know which surfaces can remain free and which areas can support additional elements.</p><h2>Plan Changes in Stages</h2><p>Test the layout with what you already own before spending money. Move a piece of furniture, remove an unnecessary object, or mark proposed dimensions on the floor. Living with a temporary arrangement for several days can reveal practical issues that are difficult to notice in a photograph.</p><p>Make functional changes first and decorate afterward. When access, storage, light, and circulation work well, decorative decisions become easier because you know which surfaces can remain free and which areas can support additional elements.</p><h2>Plan Changes in Stages</h2><p>Test the layout with what you already own before spending money. Move a piece of furniture, remove an unnecessary object, or mark proposed dimensions on the floor. Living with a temporary arrangement for several days can reveal practical issues that are difficult to notice in a photograph.</p><p>Make functional changes first and decorate afterward. When access, storage, light, and circulation work well, decorative decisions become easier because you know which surfaces can remain free and which areas can support additional elements.</p><h2>Plan Changes in Stages</h2><p>Test the layout with what you already own before spending money. Move a piece of furniture, remove an unnecessary object, or mark proposed dimensions on the floor. Living with a temporary arrangement for several days can reveal practical issues that are difficult to notice in a photograph.</p><p>Make functional changes first and decorate afterward. When access, storage, light, and circulation work well, decorative decisions become easier because you know which surfaces can remain free and which areas can support additional elements.</p><h2>Plan Changes in Stages</h2><p>Test the layout with what you already own before spending money. Move a piece of furniture, remove an unnecessary object, or mark proposed dimensions on the floor. Living with a temporary arrangement for several days can reveal practical issues that are difficult to notice in a photograph.</p><p>Make functional changes first and decorate afterward. When access, storage, light, and circulation work well, decorative decisions become easier because you know which surfaces can remain free and which areas can support additional elements.</p><h2>Plan Changes in Stages</h2><p>Test the layout with what you already own before spending money. Move a piece of furniture, remove an unnecessary object, or mark proposed dimensions on the floor. Living with a temporary arrangement for several days can reveal practical issues that are difficult to notice in a photograph.</p><p>Make functional changes first and decorate afterward. When access, storage, light, and circulation work well, decorative decisions become easier because you know which surfaces can remain free and which areas can support additional elements.</p>'}]
